r version  of it is that we're a consultancy okay  so we're there to listen to them observe  what they do listen to what their  problems are and then try to make  suggestions to them as to where they  might proceed where  might be productive for them to move  sometimes we do a little bit of work for  them but most of its it's just  transferring ideas and then of course we  get a lot we get an introduction to  Haiti the beautiful people the culture  the food we get to travel around their  country and have a nice Spring Break and  so let's talk specifics specifically  give us some examples of what you do  talk about in the in the class okay  so although we're associated with a  hospital  we actually don't go down and do any  medical work we work with an  organization called integrated community  services that the hospital set up so the  hospital like many as many hospitals do  notice that many of the people were  coming to them probably sicker than they  should be  in this case mainly because of  malnutrition so if you're malnourished  you're gonna get a cold but it's gonna  be a more serious cold or you're gonna  get cholera and it's gonna be a much  more serious version of cholera so the  hospital said we should go out into the  community and try to help solve this  malnutrition error problem and that's  where we came in so for instance as you  probably know a large amount of the  trees of Haiti have been cut down in the  last 50 years or so especially now as  the populations grown bigger the wood is  used mainly for charcoal in the rural  areas to cook mm-hmm  so we are working with this integrated  Community Services in a field called  agroforestry agroforestry is agriculture  and forestry so plants crops and trees  put together in some kind of fashion so  for instance Agri Forest we can solve  erosion problems much of Haiti is steep  hillside and if you cut the trees down  the roots aren't holding the soil bound  the soil will wash away and it will not  only degrade the farmer's land but it  will then spoil through erosion and  landslides the land down below so by  planting trees you hold the soil in  place so we are the integrated community  services already started an agroforestry  program and we're there to observe it  and help them think through problems so  one of the issues for this semester will  be yams okay one of my favorite desserts  I love them oh great especially as we  approach Thanksgiving here or around  Thanksgiving time  yams are a very nutritious plant and  they can be planted in an area where  there's a little bit more shade so you  can plant them under trees before the  canopy gets totally dark so it's you can  have trees that are growing to hold the  soil back they eventually will be a crop  that you could cut down and sell as  timber so there'll be money for the  farmer but in the meantime you can plant  the yams under the trees and either eat  them or take them to the village to sell  so that's an example of agroforestry  which they've already started and we're  just consulting with them on their  projects ok so now this will be the  fourth or fifth area going this will be  the fourth here so in the course of the  past four years what do you think have  been some of the greater successes ok so  this is a huge problem the word success  ok as you may know in the philanthropic  or foundation world the foundations  increasingly Rask proved to us that when  we give you money to do something that  you're having the desired outcomes but  if you think about it how do we measure  nutrition when we're really talking  about what a child is going to be like 3  or 4 or 5 years from now we don't know  if we really are having the effect so  it's very hard to ask the question if  we're successful the closest I've come  to that is if we come back either next  year or the year after and something  that we talked about is something that  people are doing in the field then I  will feel that we gave an idea that  someone thought was useful and that the  local people are appropriating so as an  example in our very first visit down  there we had a student from the school  public health who had been in the Peace  Corps in Africa and she'd observed  something called a tippy tap which is a  very simple hand washing mechanism that  allows you to wash your hands and the  water they that drips out of a bottle  goes into the soil so when you're  washing your hands it's it's not you're  not using water that someone else is  already used you're not washing in a tub  where your water and then you're washing  your hands and so on and so we  introduced this and there actually have  been a couple more built and the people  are talking about it and it's possible  that it will be a mechanism that well  lasting power in an area where there's  zero running water and water is very  scarce to begin with in the mountains so  since the earthquake I mean I'm not sure  how developed Haiti was prior to the  earthquake was there running water prior  to the earthquake  okay so Haiti's like most other  countries in the world including the  United States in a simple way you can  divide it into an urbanized area and a  rural area so in the urbanized area  there is running water there three-star  hotels four-star hotels there's streets  and you know bars and all everything you  would find in any city when you get to  the rural areas as you would give in the  United States you find very little  almost no piped water and everybody's  getting their water from Springs or from  wells so and and that's another problem  that we've worked on is what's the  quality of the water that's coming out  of those wells so Haiti spent a lot of  time worrying about infectious diseases  and since the earthquake we have Haiti  had the cholera that was introduced and  so washing your hands but the quality of  water used for cooking and and washing  has become a big issue so has have has  there been much improvement over the  over the 