th to me but also to a signal  to our Haitian colleagues Yael Gordon is  not going to be with us for all this  time and my hope is that they would say  ok we better make very good use of him  and tell us what not to do because we're  wasting their time and tell us what to  do where we can be helpful and back to  your one of your earlier questions  whether being where there were being  successful at that I don't know I am  gonna stick to my promise of ending in  five years so we can do five years so  two more Springs then we'll  a sixth year we will try to do the  evaluation so we'll try to you can have  me back in three more years  okay what about some of the some of the  surprises are some of the challenges you  didn't anticipate going over there with  graduate students well many of our  graduate students at Yale but especially  in public health and at the school for  environmental studies are sort of global  world travelers already lots of Peace  Corps volunteers lots of most of the  students graduate students at Yale now  have been out three or four or five  years before they come to school so they  have real-world experience and we really  can think of them as adults or maybe  young adults but very mature and so you  I knew from the beginning I wouldn't  have to do much hand holding the the  trickiest thing for them is they're used  to traveling as independent you know in  the Peace Corps they're off on their own  here they're traveling as a team they're  part of a delegation or a consulting  firm and it takes them a little while to  figure out how to work cooperatively and  take care of each other well that's I  think that's a good life lesson to learn  to work as a team and collaborate so I  think that's a good thing that's coming  out of the class yep yep welcome but  unsurprising thing is of course that  generosity generosity of the people were  that are hosting us and we're visiting I  think if you go anywhere in the world  when you talk to probably a lot o