et this is  sort of admitting there's no silver  bullets there gonna be lots of small  steps and and moving forward one of one  topic that I find fascinating is the  history of Haiti is that it was a slave  colony under the French and the slaves  fought the revolution so in in late  1700s early 1800s which is not far after  our  American Revolution here in the United  States but our Revolution was not fought  by slaves so there is a particular  there's a peculiar or particular  ambiance or understanding that I'm I  don't think I'm ever going to be able to  understand but I know it's important to  understand that the way independence was  set up was by the slaves overthrowing  the master and that still is affecting  the culture I believe so again by the  self-reliance one of the reasons I come  to self-reliance is I think the  self-reliance is a way that a slave and  enslaved people can prove to themselves  and prove to others that they're not  enslaved anymore that they've done this  themselves and even though it's a  hundred and some years later after the  Revolution because of the history  especially the relationships the United  States which have been not very good  over many years I think that this is a  very positive set of psychological  benefit but this is a person who doesn't  know a lot about Haiti talking about  other people so we academics know that  sort of a dangerous thing to do so you'd  have to ask some Haitian people I think  what they think is the next steps well  it sounds like you're doing good things  there and I thank you for being here  today and I will look forward to having  you back to wrap up thank you for having  me that's my pleasure for more  information about Professor jabal and  his work please visit our website at  Yale edu slash McMillan report be sure  to join us again for another episode of  the McMillan report made possible by the  Whitney and Betty McMillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  thank you very much 